# C2 repair option (a) (§48) — evidence

# Purpose: §47 named the mechanism that killed every shot — the fallback `offsetof` in
# libs/quickjs/cutils.h is a syntactic null member access, UBSan instruments it with the compile-time
# constant 0, and Zig's type-mismatch handler aborts, exit status 3. Repair option (a) makes
# `offsetof` well-defined (include <stddef.h> so the builtin is used). This file records the one-line
# change, the rebuilt bytes, and the zero-game verification that the artifact no longer terminates
# its own host and that `C2DiagQJSInto` now returns with `rt=` observed.
# Authorization: the human partner chose option (a) after §47. The only Windows-side actions taken
# were scp, Get-FileHash and `python` — NO game, NO injection, NO dispatch, NO CreateRemoteThread.
# exe identity: stellaris.exe 4.4.4 sha256 9242c40abb78b53e88039ead1270ad82eb003959fb2d413c856d280ea02b1a56 — NOT INVOLVED.
# Baseline artifact (the §45–§47 subject): stellaris_quickjs.dll sha256
#   77c0c35ec5b74abc8ccb849515621aaf53abd3fdc0a5e6ab8b9466acc0c11ad0 (still present at
#   .zig-cache/o/fbbf28541f873879215d186f417e32a9/stellaris_quickjs.dll, byte-identical control).
# Artifact of record (this fix): stellaris_quickjs.dll sha256
#   79c8dc240ef87d3bc979efb78e3549b937b909d57939eb552832be0d1c5c230c, deployed as C:\temp\sqjs_s48b.dll
#   and verified remotely: Get-FileHash C:\temp\sqjs_s48b.dll → 79C8DC240EF87D3BC979EFB78E3549B937B909D57939EB552832BE0D1C5C230C
# Scripts: scripts/c2_selfabort_repro.py (maintained zero-game repro), scripts/dll_ubsan_site.py
#   (offline UBSan record reader), scripts/pe_disrva.py (artifact disassembler).

## (1) The change

    libs/quickjs/cutils.h:31
    +#include <stddef.h> /* MinGW defines no offsetof; the fallback below is a null member access */

The `#ifndef offsetof` fallback at cutils.h:43-44 stays verbatim; the include only ensures it is never
the definition that takes effect.

## (2) What the preprocessor now expands, old vs new

Measured with the build's target and C flags (copies under /tmp, worktree not used as input):

    $ cp libs/quickjs/quickjs.c libs/quickjs/*.h /tmp/qjs_probe_old/     # then overwrite cutils.h:
    $ git show HEAD:libs/quickjs/cutils.h > /tmp/qjs_probe_old/cutils.h
    $ cp libs/quickjs/*.c libs/quickjs/*.h /tmp/qjs_probe_new/
    $ zig cc -target x86_64-windows-gnu -DCONFIG_VERSION='"2025-09-13"' \
        -Wno-gnu-zero-variadic-macro-arguments -E /tmp/qjs_probe_<v>/quickjs.c | grep -A2 'js_rc'

    old (HEAD):
    static inline JSMallocBlockHeader *js_rc(void *ptr)
    {
        return ((JSMallocBlockHeader *)((uint8_t *)(ptr) - ((size_t) &((JSMallocBlockHeader *)0)->user_data)));

    new (working tree):
    static inline JSMallocBlockHeader *js_rc(void *ptr)
    {
        return ((JSMallocBlockHeader *)((uint8_t *)(ptr) - __builtin_offsetof(JSMallocBlockHeader, user_data)));

The old body contains the null member access §47 localized; the new body has no expression that UBSan's
null check can attach to. The value (8) is unchanged.

## (3) The Zig build-cache trap, measured

Editing a header alone did NOT invalidate the C compile in the existing cache:

    $ zig build --summary all          # after the cutils.h edit, default cache
    ...
    +- compile lib stellaris_quickjs Debug x86_64-windows cached
    $ sha256sum zig-out/bin/stellaris_quickjs.dll
    77c0c35e...                        # unchanged — stale bytes, the defect still present

    $ zig build --cache-dir /tmp/zig-cache-offsetof --summary all
    ...
    +- compile lib stellaris_quickjs Debug x86_64-windows success
    $ sha256sum zig-out/bin/stellaris_quickjs.dll
    e9a649e4...                        # first genuinely rebuilt bytes

The artifact of record was rebuilt after the final header wording was fixed, in its own fresh cache:

    $ zig build --cache-dir /tmp/zig-cache-offsetof3 --summary all
    Build Summary: 5/5 steps succeeded
    +- install stellaris_quickjs success
    |  +- compile lib stellaris_quickjs Debug x86_64-windows success 13s MaxRSS:409M
    $ sha256sum zig-out/bin/stellaris_quickjs.dll
    79c8dc240ef87d3bc979efb78e3549b937b909d57939eb552832be0d1c5c230c

Operational consequence for the fix loop: after a header-only edit, check the compile step's status
line and the sha. A "cached" C compile would scp the OLD bytes to the host, and the repro would still
show the abort — a false negative for the fix. Use a fresh `--cache-dir` for header-only changes until
this is understood better. (A second consequence, in `scripts/test_pe_disrva.py`, is §48.2's note: the
tests had pinned the old trap span and now derive it from the artifact via `trap_span()`.)

## (4) Offline confirmation on the new bytes

    $ python3 scripts/dll_ubsan_site.py zig-out/bin/stellaris_quickjs.dll site JSMallocBlockHeader
    [ubsan_site] 133 UBSan check sites for a type containing 'JSMallocBlockHeader'
    $ python3 scripts/dll_ubsan_site.py zig-out/bin/stellaris_quickjs.dll site JSMallocBlockHeader \
        | grep -c "quickjs.c:1494:12"
    0

    baseline control (77c0c35e..., same command):
    [ubsan_site] 141 UBSan check sites for a type containing 'JSMallocBlockHeader'
    grep -c "quickjs.c:1494:12" → 1

The 8 removed records are exactly the 8 `container_of` expressions in quickjs.c that are built from
the fallback `offsetof`, line for line:

    1494  js_rc                       container_of(ptr, JSMallocBlockHeader, user_data)
    1601  __js_free                   container_of(ptr, JSMallocBlockHeader, user_data)
    1607  __js_free                   JSMallocLargeBlockHeader  (header.user_data variant)
    1645  __js_realloc                container_of(ptr, JSMallocBlockHeader, user_data)
    1651  __js_realloc                JSMallocLargeBlockHeader  (header.user_data variant)
    1684  __js_realloc                container_of(new_ptr, JSMallocBlockHeader, user_data)
    1704  __js_malloc_usable_size     container_of(ptr, JSMallocBlockHeader, user_data)
    1711  __js_malloc_usable_size     JSMallocLargeBlockHeader  (header.user_data variant)

(The 1607/1651/1711 lines are `JSMallocLargeBlockHeader`, whose spelling contains the searched
substring.) So the include removes the instrumentation from **all eight** fallback uses, not just
`js_rc`. Only `js_rc` was on the exercised path; the seven in the free/realloc/usable-size paths were
latent copies of the same unconditional abort that the first call down any of those paths would have
hit. This is why the one-line include is preferred over special-casing `js_rc`.

The remaining nearby records (`quickjs.c:1477:29`, `1522:12`, `1523:12`, `1526:8`, `1527:8`) are
ordinary implicit null checks on runtime pointers (`s->zero_size_block`, `get_arena_block(...)`
results), not compile-time constants; they report only if the pointer is actually null. No export was
lost (`C2DiagMfRecordsInto`, `C2DiagQJSInto`, `C2Sanity`, `C2Diag`, … all present in the export
table).

